Fixed and committed as c09255ec. With the reproduction from #1049, yoyo -p now exits in 6.6s instead of 34.5s, and the backgrounded sleep keeps running after it exits. Format, clippy and the full test suite all pass.

The cause was the one #1049 suspected. After bash exited, the tool gave its output reader 2 seconds to finish. When that ran out, the code dropped the reader's handle, which leaves the task running in the background instead of stopping it. That task kept reading the pipe the orphan held open, and -p didn't exit until the orphan did. Now, when the 2 seconds run out, the reader is stopped and its end of the pipe closed before the result goes back, and output already read is kept. The reader only does async reads, so stopping it was enough. I didn't find out why a leftover async task blocked process exit; only this one change sits between the two measurements.

Measured with the real provider (anthropic, claude-opus-5-5) against target/debug/yoyo:
• Before: 34.5s wall.
• After: 6.6s wall.

One behaviour change: a background job whose output isn't redirected now dies with SIGPIPE the next time it writes after those 2 seconds. Before, its output went into a buffer nobody read, and -p never exited. Jobs started like server >log 2>&1 & are unaffected. I added a short section about this to docs/src/troubleshooting/common-issues.md.

Tests: five real-spawn tests, in src/tools_group_kill_tests.rs next to the existing process-group tests rather than in tools.rs's own test module:
• The #1049 command returns hi in under 10s, and the orphan is still alive afterwards.
• A write from the orphan after the tool returns must fail. As a positive control I put back the old line, and only this test failed; then I restored the fix.
• Three cases that must not change: a redirected orphan gives exactly the same result, slow output from commands that all exit keeps both lines, and orphan output arriving within the 2 seconds is kept.

tools.rs is now 5240 lines, which the size gate still accepts, so its register entry didn't need editing.

scripts/evolve.sh runs yoyo -p under timeout for every phase, so my own loop is affected; this can only make those runs finish sooner.

Not fixed here: sub-agents use yoagent's own bash tool (src/tools.rs:1744), not this one. From reading yoagent 0.25.7, it waits for both pipes to close before checking whether bash exited. With the same command it would probably wait until its timeout and then kill the orphan. I haven't run it, and I filed it as #1051 with the upstream label.
